		Page numbering: restart
		First number: 87
		Block:
			Id: opener
			Text: Chapter 4
				Style: chapter-number
			Heading 1:
				Text: Orchard and Field
			Paragraph: October is when the kitchen slows down. The squash have hardened their skins in the field, the late pears are finally soft enough to eat, and the walnuts have dropped onto the lane by the gate. These two recipes use all three, and both are better made a day ahead.
				Style: intro
				Drop cap: 3
			Paragraph: Neither asks for much: an oven, a heavy pan and some patience while the butter turns brown or the cake cools. Buy squash that feel heavy for their size, and pears that give a little at the neck.
				Style: intro-more
			Figure:
				Id: fig-pears
				Caption: Comice and Conference pears, with walnuts from the lane.
				Image: documents/recipes/pears.svg
					Alt: Drawing of two pears, one yellow and one russet, with two walnuts
					Box:
						Width: 62%
			Text: In this chapter
				Style: list-label
			Table of contents:
				Id: recipes-toc
				Levels: 2 2
		Block:
			Id: squash
			Style: recipe
			Heading 2:
				Text: Roast squash with sage brown butter
			Stack:
				Style: details
				Item:
					Text: Serves 4
						Style: detail-main
				Item:
					Text: Prep 15 min
						Style: detail
				Item:
					Text: Oven 45 min at 200 °C
						Style: detail
			Paragraph: A whole tray of squash, roasted until the edges catch, then dressed with butter cooked to the colour of hazelnuts and crisp sage leaves. Serve it with bread for the butter, or next to a roast chicken.
				Style: lead
			Grid:
				Style: recipe-grid
				Area:
					Block:
						Style: ingredients
						Text: Ingredients
							Style: list-label
						List:
							Style: leaves
							List item:
								Text: 1 kg squash (onion or butternut)
							List item:
								Text: 2 tbsp olive oil
							List item:
								Text: 1 tsp flaky salt
							List item:
								Text: 60 g butter
							List item:
								Text: 16 sage leaves
							List item:
								Text: 1 small garlic clove
							List item:
								Text: ½ lemon
							List item:
								Text: 40 g hazelnuts, toasted
				Area:
					Block:
						Text: Method
							Style: list-label
						List:
							Style: steps
							List item:
								Paragraph: Heat the oven to 200 °C. Halve the squash, scoop out the seeds and cut it into wedges about 3 cm thick. There is no need to peel an onion squash; the skin softens as it roasts.
							List item:
								Paragraph: Toss the wedges with the oil and salt on a large tray, in a single layer. Roast for 40 to 45 minutes, turning once, until soft and dark at the edges.
							List item:
								Paragraph: Meanwhile, melt the butter in a light-coloured pan over a medium heat. When it foams, add the sage and the sliced garlic and swirl until the butter smells of nuts and the leaves are crisp, about 3 minutes.
							List item:
								Paragraph: Take the pan off the heat and squeeze in the lemon. Spoon the butter and leaves over the squash and scatter with the chopped hazelnuts.
			Heading 3:
				Text: Cook’s notes
			Image: documents/recipes/squash.svg
				Style: side-drawing
				Side: right
			Paragraph: The butter is ready when the solids at the bottom of the pan turn golden brown and the foam settles. A light pan shows the colour; in a dark one it is easy to go too far, and burnt butter tastes bitter.
			Paragraph: Onion squash and butternut roast in the same time. A pumpkin grown for carving is too watery for this: it steams instead of browning.
			Paragraph: The squash keeps for two days in the fridge. Warm it in the oven and make the butter fresh.
			Block:
				Style: variations
				Text: Variations
					Style: list-label
				Columns:
					Style: variation-columns
					Paragraph: **With blue cheese.** Crumble 80 g of a soft blue over the hot squash just before the butter goes on.
					Paragraph: **With lentils.** Fold the squash through 250 g of cooked green lentils and a spoonful of mustard for a main course.
					Paragraph: **With chilli.** Add a pinch of chilli flakes to the butter with the sage.
					Paragraph: **Without dairy.** Fry the sage in olive oil and finish it with the lemon and a little honey.
		Block:
			Id: cake
			Style: recipe
			Heading 2:
				Text: Pear and walnut cake
			Stack:
				Style: details
				Item:
					Text: Makes one 22 cm cake
						Style: detail-main
				Item:
					Text: Prep 25 min
						Style: detail
				Item:
					Text: Oven 50 min at 170 °C
						Style: detail
			Paragraph: A plain, damp cake with pears pressed into the top. It is at its best the day after baking, when the crumb has taken on the pear juice.
				Style: lead
			Figure:
				Id: cake-quantities
				Caption: Quantities for a 22 cm round tin and for a 900 g loaf tin.
				Table:
					Style: quantities
					Header rows:
						Row:
							Cell: Ingredient
							Cell: Round tin
							Cell: Loaf tin
					Body rows:
						Row:
							Cell: Ripe pears
								Row header: true
							Cell: 3
							Cell: 2
						Row:
							Cell: Soft butter
								Row header: true
							Cell: 150 g
							Cell: 110 g
						Row:
							Cell: Light brown sugar
								Row header: true
							Cell: 140 g
							Cell: 100 g
						Row:
							Cell: Eggs, at room temperature
								Row header: true
							Cell: 3
							Cell: 2
						Row:
							Cell: Plain flour
								Row header: true
							Cell: 120 g
							Cell: 90 g
						Row:
							Cell: Ground walnuts
								Row header: true
							Cell: 80 g
							Cell: 60 g
						Row:
							Cell: Baking powder
								Row header: true
							Cell: 2 tsp
							Cell: 1½ tsp
						Row:
							Cell: Salt and a pinch of cinnamon
								Row header: true
							Cell: the same for both
								Style: span-note
								Span: 1 2
			Text: Method
				Style: list-label
			Columns:
				Style: method-columns
				List:
					Style: steps
					List item:
						Paragraph: Heat the oven to 170 °C and line the tin with baking paper. Peel and quarter the pears and take out the cores.
					List item:
						Paragraph: Beat the butter and sugar until pale, about 4 minutes. Add the eggs one at a time, with a spoonful of the flour after each one so the mixture does not split.
					List item:
						Paragraph: Fold in the rest of the flour, the walnuts, the baking powder, the salt and the cinnamon. Spread the batter in the tin.
					List item:
						Paragraph: Press the pear quarters into the top, cut side down. Bake for 50 minutes, until a skewer comes out clean, and leave the cake in its tin for 15 minutes before turning it out.
			Block:
				Style: tip
				Paragraph: **Hard pears?** Poach the quarters in syrup for 5 minutes first.
